==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|fr|-}}, from {{uder|en|la|-}} {{lena}} {{m|la|officīna||a workshop}}, contracted from {{m|la|opificīna}}, from {{m|la|opifex||a workman}}; {{m|la|opus||work}} + {{m|la|faciō||to make or do}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˌɒfɪˈsaɪnəl/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-officinal.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪnəl|s=4}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# {{lb|en|dated}} [[medicinal|Medicinal]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete|rare}} Used in a [[shop]], or belonging to it.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1786| title=The Natural History of Many Curious and Uncommon Zoophytes|author=John Ellis; Daniel Charles Solander | page=| passage=Millepora polymorpha: British '''officinal''' Coral. {{...}} This is the Coral of the Shops, and is found in great plenty in the seas round these islands, especially near Falmouth, and the Isle of Man.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|pharmaceutical}} Kept in stock by [[apothecary|apothecaries]]; said of such drugs and medicines as may be obtained without special preparation or compounding; not [[magistral]].

